body:
Thanks @markstory. In the past I thought something was buggy with how BTM data with _joinData works, and it seems like when trying to turn this into a behavior I found a consistent bug.. since it's related to this I'll put it here (if you want me to open a new issue I can).

Trying to save BTM after something already exists in the DB, using _joinData, and an alias as the association name, it doesn't save the new data with _joinData.
In my `PostsTable`

```
$this->addAssociations([
    'belongsToMany' => [
        'PrimaryTags' => [
            'className' => 'Tags',
            'targetForeignKey' => 'tag_id',
            'conditions' => ['primary' => 1]
        ]
    ]
]);
```

Controller

```
$tags = [];
if (!empty($this->request->data('primary_tags._ids'))) {
    foreach ($this->request->data('primary_tags._ids') as $tagid) {
        $tags[] = ['id' => $tagid, '_joinData' => ['primary' => 1]];
    }
}
$this->request->data('primary_tags', $tags);
```

Data

```
[primary_tags] => Array
(
    [_ids] => Array
        (
            [0] => 141
        )

)
```

If you have an empty `posts_tags` table and save that once, it works. Try it again with the same data and you get two records in the DB: one with primary=1, one with primary=0. Save any other IDs, that have already been saved with primary=1 and there will be duplicated records.

I think if this is fixed I should be on my way to Behavior-ifying this whole process. I'd rather not add logic to delete all the `posts_tags` because it seems like this should work without that.

Thanks!

Also, another fun little one which might be related (I'm trying to track this down), why does `AssociationCollection::keys` return `primarytags` (sic) for my `PrimaryTags` association? That means you have to explicitly specify `primary_tags` when patching an entity if your data has an entity in it:

```
$post = $this->Posts->get($id);
$data = [
    'primary_tags' => [
        '_ids' => [1, 2]
    ]
];
foreach ($data['primary_tags']['_ids'] as $tagid) {
    $tag = $this->Posts->PrimaryTags->get($tagid);
    $tag->_joinData = $this->Posts->PrimaryTags->PostsTags->findOrCreate(['primary' => 1]);
    $tags[] = $tag;
}
$data['primary_tags'] = $tags;
$this->Posts->patchEntity($post, $data, ['associated' => ['primary_tags']]);
pr($post);
```

Cake thinks the key for `PrimaryTags` is `primarytags`. I'm not sure what the `AssociationCollection::keys` are used for, but why are they different from the key your data from an association constructs? (Sorry if that's a weird question, makes sense in my head.)

In the code above, if I don't specify the `associated` array, then my `$post` doesn't have `primary_tags` on it, when it should even without specifying the array (since Cake by default attempts to add all the associations).

Edited for clarity.

OK, so, things work normally if I update `AssociationCollection` to use `Inflector::underscore` instead of `strtolower` for the aliases and if I add `['associated' => ['primary_tags._joinData']]` to my `patchEntity` call. Does that change sound like something that makes sense? Ideally shouldn't it use the association's property name? `strtolower` definitely can't work for camelcased aliases since the data for those associations uses `assoc::property()` as the key.

```
public function add($alias, Association $association)
{
    list(, $alias) = pluginSplit($alias);        
    return $this->_items[$association->property()] = $association;
}
```

I think this is where the confusion comes in, Cake works if you don't add _joinData because it marshals correctly, but if that association has _joinData, you need to first change it's key in the data that you send or be explicit with the `associated` key..

If you do this

```
$post = $this->Posts->get($id);
$data = [
    'primary_tags' => [
        '_ids' => [1,2,3]
    ]
];
$this->Posts->patchEntity($post, $data);
$this->Posts->save($post);
```

Then this

```
$post = $this->Posts->get($id);
$data = [
    'primary_tags' => [
        '_ids' => [1]
    ]
];
$this->Posts->patchEntity($post, $data);
$this->Posts->save($post);
```

You'll still have three records in the DB, when you should have one, right? (bug).

If you do this

```
$post = $this->Posts->get($id);
$data = [
    'primary_tags' => [
        '_ids' => [1,2,3]
    ]
];
$ptags = [];
foreach ($data['primary_tags']['_ids'] as $tagid) {
    $ptags[] = ['id' => $tagid, '_joinData' => ['primary' => 1]];
}
$data['primary_tags'] = $ptags;
$this->Posts->patchEntity($post, $data);
$this->Posts->save($post);
```

Then this:

```
$post = $this->Posts->get($id);
$data = [
    'primary_tags' => [
        '_ids' => [1]
    ]
];
$ptags = [];
foreach ($data['primary_tags']['_ids'] as $tagid) {
    $ptags[] = ['id' => $tagid, '_joinData' => ['primary' => 1]];
}
$data['primary_tags'] = $ptags;
$this->Posts->patchEntity($post, $data);
$this->Posts->save($post);
```

You'll have two records in the DB (one with primary=0, one with primary=1) (another bug).

The only way it works correctly is if you do this:

```
$post = $this->Posts->get($id);
$data = [
    'primary_tags' => [
        '_ids' => [1]
    ]
];
$ptags = [];
foreach ($data['primary_tags']['_ids'] as $tagid) {
    $ptags[] = ['id' => $tagid, '_joinData' => ['primary' => 1]];
}
$data['primary_tags'] = $ptags;
$this->Posts->patchEntity($post, $data, ['associated' => ['PrimaryTags._joinData']]);
$this->Posts->save($post);
```

It seems like Cake wants to work without the `associated` key, but it fails the second time, possibly when it checks if the join'd record exists, since the existence needs to be based on conditions.

Hey @markstory thanks for your help with this. I've been afk for the holidays but looked at this again today. Forgetting about bug1 for now since that might just be me being stupid, this is just for bug2..

In my `PostsTable` I have an overridden `findAll` which adds a contain, and that seems to be what's confusing things. In your shell, I changed it to 

```
$id = 50;
$bookmarks = TableRegistry::get('Bookmarks');
$bookmark = $bookmarks->get($id, ['contain' => ['StarredTags']]);
$data = [
    'starred_tags' => [
        ['id' => 1, '_joinData' => ['starred' => 1]],
        ['id' => 2, '_joinData' => ['starred' => 1]],
        ['id' => 3, '_joinData' => ['starred' => 1]],
    ]
];
$bookmarks->patchEntity($bookmark, $data);
$bookmarks->save($bookmark);

$bookmark = $bookmarks->get($id, ['contain' => ['StarredTags']]);
$data = [
    'starred_tags' => [
        ['id' => 1, '_joinData' => ['starred' => 1]]
    ]
];
$bookmarks->patchEntity($bookmark, $data);
$bookmarks->save($bookmark);
```

Running that once, it's fine. There's a single record in `bookmarks_tags` like you'd expect. However, running that twice results in there being three records in `bookmarks_tags`, two with `starred=0`.

The only way I can see to fix it is to change the `patchEntity` and add the `associated` part.

```
$bookmarks->patchEntity($bookmark, $data, ['associated' => ['StarredTags._joinData']]);
```

My gut is telling me that's a bug, because why does it work once without the `associated` part but not a second time?
